parts = [f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>MultiSearch — implementation and feasibility evidence</title><style>{css}</style></head><body>
<header><div class="eyebrow">MILVUS ENGINEERING PROTOTYPE · 31 OCTOBER 2026</div><h1>MultiSearch<br>Independent searches. One RPC.</h1><p>A working Proxy implementation, a rebuilt server binary, real client calls, and measured comparisons with ordinary Search.</p><span class="tag">Standalone verified</span> <span class="tag">{'Kubernetes verified' if k8s_ready else 'Kubernetes validation pending'}</span></header>
<main><nav><a href="#verdict">Finding</a><a href="#design">Design</a><a href="#code">Key code</a><a href="#correctness">Functional evidence</a><a href="#performance">Performance</a><a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a><a href="#release">Release work</a><a href="#reproduce">Reproduce</a></nav>
<section id="verdict"><h2>The idea is feasible at the Proxy boundary</h2><p>The implemented API accepts a bag of complete, independent Search requests. Proxy applies the normal Search entry checks to each item, invokes its ordinary execution path, and returns separate results. The prototype successfully searched collections with different schemas, dimensions, metrics and topK values in one external RPC.</p>
<div class="cards"><div class="card"><strong>50 → 1</strong>external RPCs per retrieval group<br><small>Measured by the client interceptor</small></div><div class="card"><strong>24</strong>heterogeneous collections<br><small>6,144 populated rows per environment</small></div><div class="card"><strong>{p['group_p50_ms']/m['group_p50_ms']:.2f}×</strong>lower median group latency<br><small>Local microbenchmark, concurrency 1</small></div></div>
<p>This directly resolves the schema/metric objection in the discussion: a unified cross-collection ranking would require compatible scores and a defined merge operation. MultiSearch does neither. Each item validates its own collection and performs its own normal shard-level reduction. There is no reduce across items and no atomic cross-collection snapshot.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Evidence supports proceeding to API design review.</strong> The implementation and end-to-end tests demonstrate that this orchestration can live in Proxy without changes to QueryNode, Segcore, indexes or WAL behavior. Production readiness, customer latency targets and PSC incident mitigation require additional validation.</div>
</section>
<section id="design"><h2>Contract and execution path</h2>
<div class="flow"><div class="box"><strong>Client</strong>One MultiSearch RPC<br>50 complete Search requests</div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="box"><strong>Proxy</strong>Authenticate envelope<br>Validate and admit batch<br>Bounded worker pool<br>Each item: Search entry checks → Search → dqQueue</div><div class="arrow">→</div><div class="box"><strong>Existing execution</strong>Normal routing and QueryNode work<br>Separate SearchResults per item</div></div>
<p>The fan-out happens in process, into the existing Proxy Search entry path. No loopback network calls are introduced. The dispatcher has no knowledge of schemas, shards, indexes, or score merging. Each item keeps its own nq and quota accounting; packing collections together does not turn them into one larger-nq collection search. Vector and result payloads still have to cross the network.</p>
''',table(['Decision','Prototype behavior'],[
('API name','MultiSearch; Python adapter method multi_search(...)'),
('Wire entry','/milvus.proto.multisearch.MultiSearchService/MultiSearch on the existing Proxy gRPC port'),
('Request','One database plus ordered {id, SearchRequest} items; repeated collections allowed'),
('Independence','Different fields, vector dimensions, metrics, filters, topK and output fields per item'),
('Result','Ordered {id, SearchResults} or per-item gRPC code/message; envelope success does not mean all items succeeded'),
('Partial failure','Ordinary item failures preserve successful siblings; no automatic retries'),
('Whole-envelope failure','Invalid envelope, request/response size, admission rejection, or overall deadline; no partial-results recovery on a lost unary response'),
('Database','Envelope DB → connection metadata → default. Conflicting item DBs rejected before dispatch'),
('Consistency','Each item retains ordinary Search consistency. No cross-item snapshot or atomicity')]),
'''<h3>Entry checks must run per item</h3><p>Calling Proxy.Search directly would bypass network interceptors. This implementation refactors the existing Search entry chain into a shared function, then explicitly runs it for every item using Search's real method name and request type. That preserves database handling, collection RBAC, per-item quotas, hooks, access logging and ordinary standalone forwarding. The envelope has its own explicit authentication/access chain; it does not rely on permissive fallbacks for unknown request types.</p><p>Requests are cloned before dispatch. Mutable RPC statistics and access-log state are isolated per item; identity, deadline and trace ancestry are inherited. Cloud-only gateway and billing hooks are not available in this OSS environment and remain a release gate.</p>''',
 table(['Guardrail','Default','Scope'], [('Batch size','128 items','Validated before dispatch'),('Request size','4 MiB','Serialized envelope'),('Per-batch concurrency','16','Independent Search callbacks'),('Global concurrency','64','MultiSearch items per Proxy; ordinary Search keeps existing controls'),('Active envelopes','8','Fail-fast admission, no unbounded waiting batches'),('Response budget','16 MiB','Retained serialized outcomes; not a bound on all temporary native allocations'),('Maximum duration','30 seconds','Earlier client deadline wins')]),
'''<h3>What batching changes about failures</h3><p>Under the illustrative assumption of independent 3% failures per external RPC, the chance of at least one failure in a group is <code>1 − (1 − 0.03)<sup>n</sup></code>: 78.2% for 50 RPCs and 95.2% for 100. A single envelope has 3% exposure in that simplified model; two envelopes have 5.91%, and three have 8.73%.</p><p>These are model calculations, not measured PSC behavior. Real network failures may be correlated, and larger payloads or longer requests may change failure probability. A failed envelope affects all its items. Adaptive model search rounds still need separate calls. Internal search failures and total database work do not disappear.</p><p>Returning partial outcomes does not relax the application’s requirement that every required search succeed. It makes the failed subset visible. A client can later retry only failed items when it receives a usable response, subject to an overall deadline, backoff and a single retry owner. Losing the entire response can require replaying the bag and repeating successful work. This prototype deliberately adds no retry policy.</p></section>
<section id="code"><h2>The code that demonstrates the design</h2><p>These excerpts are extracted from the implemented files. The complete protocol and dispatcher are small enough to review directly; generated protobuf bindings and the existing Search engine provide the rest.</p>''',
code('pkg/proto/multisearch.proto'),
'''<h3>Re-enter ordinary Search at the correct boundary</h3><p>The callback uses the same entry chain as network Search calls, then calls Server.Search. This is the key difference between a safe orchestration primitive and an internal loop that skips authorization or quotas.</p>''',
code('internal/distributed/proxy/multisearch.go','\tentry := proxyEntryInterceptor(limiter)','\n\treturn &multiSearchServer'),
'''<h3>Bounded workers, isolated requests, independent outcomes</h3>''',
code('internal/proxy/multisearch.go','\tfor range min(e.limits.Parallelism','\n\twg.Wait()'),
'''<h3>The client really sends one request</h3><p>The local adapter reuses PyMilvus's existing Search messages and adds one extension service stub. This is a functioning prototype method, not a claim that released MilvusClient already supports MultiSearch.</p>''',
code('examples/multisearch/client.py','    def multi_search(', '\n    def search('),
'''<p>Public protocol ownership is intentionally separated for this prototype: the extension lives in the Milvus repository and imports the existing public Search messages. Graduation requires an agreed public protobuf/API location, SDK support and compatibility behavior.</p></section>
<section id="correctness"><h2>Functional validation before performance</h2><p>The final binary ran in a standalone Docker container with authentication enabled. The final standalone verification reused the deterministic fixture populated by the Python harness: 24 collections × 256 rows, vector dimensions 8/16/32, metrics L2/IP/COSINE, differing field names, integer/string primary keys, FLAT indexes and strong consistency. A 50-item MultiSearch was compared with 50 ordinary Search calls by IDs, per-query topKs, scalar output fields and scores (tolerance 1e−5).</p>''',
checks(standalone['tests']),
'''<h3>Additional real-server boundary and quota checks</h3>''',checks(limits),
'''<p>The quota test temporarily set one collection's search quota to zero, observed the same status from ordinary Search and the corresponding bag item, and verified that a permitted sibling succeeded. The quota was restored before the final benchmark. This exercises the real rate-limit path, not a mocked callback.</p>
<h3>Failure-path audit and verification scope</h3>''',
table(['Failure / invariant','Evidence','Boundary of the claim'],[
('Missing collection','Live Search and MultiSearch return code 100','Existing lookup/status preserved'),
('Wrong vector dimension','Live result code 2000; reason includes Segcore code 2001','Existing upstream classification is preserved, not corrected'),
('Collection permission denied','Live per-item gRPC code 7; allowed sibling succeeds','Public OSS RBAC exercised'),
('Invalid credentials','Live envelope gRPC code 16','Authentication before dispatch'),
('Deadline','Live client code 4 followed by a successful request; unit tests stop queued dispatch','No guarantee of immediate termination of all native work'),
('Quota zero','Live code 9, retriable=false, matching ordinary Search','Zero-quota denial tested; every other overload origin is not fault-injected'),
('Admission / concurrency','Focused unit and race tests','Limits and slot release tested with controlled callbacks'),
('Response overflow / nil result','Focused unit tests','Not a live-server memory exhaustion test'),
('Mutable statistics','Concurrent race test','Independent request state verified'),
('Existing Proxy service','Test_NewServer regression subtests pass with disposable etcd','Focused regression, not full repository test suite')]),
'''<p>The relevant status paths were inspected at their origins and consumers: metadata lookup, Segcore dimension validation, authentication/privilege interception, quota denial and deadline propagation. No generic error reclassification or retry behavior was added. Object-storage throttling, corruption, OOM, node crashes, PSC jitter and lost responses were not injected. Their recovery behavior is not claimed.</p>
<details><summary>Test execution notes and discarded runs</summary><p>The final evidence is <code>results.json</code>, <code>limits-results.json</code>, <code>performance.json</code> and the final test logs. One intermediate benchmark was invalidated because a quota fault-injection test ran concurrently; the quota was restored and functional checks and performance were rerun separately. Initial unit-test environment issues (missing etcd and an unwritable default storage directory) were resolved with a dedicated etcd and a temporary local data path. A broader existing parameter test assumes a hardcoded storage path and fails under that override; the focused Proxy configuration and merr guard checks pass. The full repository test suite was not run.</p></details></section>
<section id="performance"><h2>Performance against ordinary Search</h2><p>Each measured group performs the same 50 logical searches. Baselines use a persistent gRPC channel: sequential Search for reference and bounded client fan-out as the useful comparison. Parallel Search and MultiSearch both allow 16 searches per group and 64 active searches globally. There are no retries. Warmup is excluded; every returned item is checked for success.</p>
<p>Standalone Docker was limited to 16 CPUs and 32 GiB on a 128-logical-CPU host. Measurements were taken before the Kubernetes workload was deployed. Client CPU is Python process CPU; server CPU is the standalone container's cgroup CPU delta, including its background activity.</p>
<div class="interactive"><label for="concurrency">Concurrent retrieval groups: </label><select id="concurrency"><option value="1">1</option><option value="4">4</option><option value="8">8</option></select><p class="muted">Median time to complete all 50 searches · lower is better</p><div id="bars"></div></div>
''',performance_table,cpu_table,
f'''<div class="note"><strong>Observed at concurrency 1:</strong> median completion time fell from {p['group_p50_ms']:.2f} ms to {m['group_p50_ms']:.2f} ms ({(1-m['group_p50_ms']/p['group_p50_ms'])*100:.1f}% lower), with {m['logical_searches_per_s']/p['logical_searches_per_s']:.2f}× the completed logical-search throughput. Client CPU per logical search fell by {(1-m['client_cpu_s']/p['client_cpu_s'])*100:.1f}%. External RPC count fell 50×; the number of logical Search operations did not.</div>
<div class="note caveat"><strong>Interpret this as an orchestration microbenchmark.</strong> The dataset is small, searches are cheap, and Python client overhead is prominent. Each parallel/batch phase contains only 60 groups; high-concurrency MultiSearch phases last under one second. The p99 values are descriptive sample percentiles, not stable tail estimates. The baseline is this Python gRPC client, not the customer’s Baseten C runtime / REST client. This is neither sustained saturation capacity nor evidence of production speedup, server efficiency at scale, or a PSC reliability fix.</div>
<p>All seven final phases completed: {sum(x['logical_searches'] for x in phases):,} measured logical searches, zero observed errors. The likely mechanism is reduced Python/gRPC work and moving fan-out into the Go Proxy; the experiment does not isolate each cost with profiling. Larger vectors, ANN indexes, filters, larger outputs, heterogeneous slow items and a remote network should be tested next. A unary envelope still waits for all items, so a slow item controls completion latency.</p></section>
<section id="kubernetes"><h2>Distributed Kubernetes functional validation</h2>''']
